@node Naming system
@section Naming system

In SCI, variable names use @code{case-insensitive} naming (conventionally @code{lowerCase} or @code{upperCase} for globals).
All keywords are case-insensitive but conventionally written in lowercase.

@table @asis
@item Keywords: (if), (else), (then), (while), (for), (foreach), (break), (continue), (return), (define), (setq), (set), (get), (put), (call), (proc), (method), (class), (instance), (send), (super), (self), (print), (format), (strcat), (strlen), (substr), (upcase), (downcase), (numtostr), (strtonum), (+), (-), (*), (/), (=), (<>), (<), (>), (<=), (>=), (and), (or), (not)
@item Operators: Same as keywords for arithmetic and logical operations
@item String delimiters: Double quotes (@code{"..."}) for strings, single quotes (@code{'...'}) for symbols or characters
@item Comments: Semicolon (;) to end of line
@end table

@node Basic structure
@section Basic structure for a stand-alone routine or program

An SCI program consists of procedure and method definitions, and a main entry point that is called by the interpreter.

@example
; Initialize game
(define gScore 0)
(define gLivesLeft 3)
(define gCurrentRoom 1)

(procedure (InitializeGame)
  (= gScore 0)
  (= gLivesLeft 3)
  (= gCurrentRoom 1)
)

(procedure (ProcessInput)
  ; Simulate input handling
  (if (Said '/?move/?north')
      (= gCurrentRoom (+ gCurrentRoom 10)))
)

(procedure (UpdateGame)
  ; Game logic updates
)

(procedure (RenderDisplay)
  ; Rendering code
)

(instance gGame of Game
  (properties)
  (method (init)
    (InitializeGame)
    (= gGameOver FALSE)
    (while (not gGameOver)
      (ProcessInput)
      (UpdateGame)
      (RenderDisplay))
  )
)
@end example

@node Basic structure object
@section Basic structure for an object method

SCI supports object-oriented programming through classes and instances, with methods defined in the class or instance.

@example
; Define a Player class
(class CPlayer of BaseViewer
  (properties
    x 0
    y 0
    sprite 0)
  (method (init theX theY theSprite)
    (= x theX)
    (= y theY)
    (= sprite theSprite)
    (super init:))
  (method (changePos dx dy)
    (= x (+ x dx))
    (= y (+ y dy)))
)

; Create instance
(instance gPlayer of CPlayer
  (properties)
)
@end example

@node Calling routines
@section Calling a routine (nullary)

To call a procedure that takes no arguments:

@example
(InitializeGraphics)
(ClearScreen)
(PlayStartupMusic)
(SomeProcedure)
@end example

@node Calling routines value
@section Calling a routine with a value (in accumulator)

To call a procedure that returns a single value (in accumulator):

@example
(= gScore (CalculateBonus gLevel gTimeLeft))
(= gBonusFactor (GetInputFactor))
(= gVolume (GetMasterVolume))
(SomeFunction)
@end example

@node Calling routines giving
@section Calling a routine giving a value (in accumulator)

Not applicable in SCI's standard calling convention.

@node Calling routines values
@section Calling a routine with values in and out (in accumulator)

To call a procedure with parameters:

@example
(DrawSprite gSpriteId gXPos gYPos)
(PlaySound gSoundId gVolume gLoopFlag)
(CheckCollision gObj1 gObj2 gResultVar)
(SomeProcedure Param1 Param2)
@end example

@node Calling library
@section Calling a library routine (all 4 styles)

SCI accesses library routines through standard procedure calls or object methods:

@example
(LoadLevel gLevelNumber)          @r{Direct call}
(FarProc gLibraryAddress)         @r{Far call}
(= gResult (LibraryFunction gInput)) @r{Library function}
(Send gObject 'Method gParam1 gParam2)  @r{Object method call}
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: ProcedureName(...)
@item Far: AbsoluteAddress(...) (via external interface)
@item Library: LibraryEntry(...) or FunctionName(...)
@item Object: Send OBJECT 'Method ...
@end table

@node Calling far bank
@section Calling a far bank Service (all 4 styles)

Far procedures in SCI are called using the FarProc kernel call or through
the export/import mechanism:

@example
; Declare far procedure
(export FarLoadLibrary from "farproc")

; Call far routine
(FarProc FarLoadLibrary #x8000)   @r{Call routine in bank 1}
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Not used for far calls
@item Far: FarProc kernel call
@item Library: Not applicable
@item Object: Not applicable
@end table

@node Invoking object
@section Invoking a method on an object (all 4 styles)

Object method invocation in SCI uses the send keyword:

@example
(Send gPlayer 'changePos 5 10)
(Send gScoreDisplay 'update gScore)
(= gGameOver (Send gGameState 'isGameOver))
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Setting object properties via direct assignment
@item Far: Not applicable for methods (objects typically in same memory)
@item Library: Method may call library routines internally
@item Object: Send OBJECT 'Method ...
@end table

@node Assignment
@section Assigning to a variable

Variables are assigned using setq or set:

@example
(setq gScore 0)                     @r{Explicit assignment}
(setq gLivesLeft 3)                 @r{Implicit assignment}
(setq gCounter (1+ gCounter))       @r{Increment}
(setq gLivesLeft (1- gLivesLeft))   @r{Decrement}
(setq gScore (+ gScore 10))         @r{Arithmetic assignment}
(setq gMessage "Hello, World!")     @r{String assignment}
@end example

@node Assignment object
@section Assigning to an object slot

Object slots (properties) are assigned using setq or set with property access:

@example
(setq gPlayer:x 160)                @r{Set object's x coordinate}
(setq gPlayer:y 100)                @r{Set object's y coordinate}
(setq gPlayer:sprite 5)             @r{Set sprite number}
(setq gPlayer:visible TRUE)         @r{Set visibility flag}
@end example

@node Assignment array
@section Assigning to an array member with a subscript

SCI uses lists and arrays. Elements are assigned using setq or set with accessor:

@example
(define gScoreList (CreateArray 10 0))
(define gInitialsList (CreateArray 10 ""))
(define gBoard (CreateArray 8 8 0))

(= (ArrayGet gScoreList 0) 100)
(= (ArrayGet gInitialsList 0) "ALI")
(= (ArrayGet gBoard 2 3) 1)
@end example

@node Arithmetic
@section Basic arithmetic: addition and subtraction

Basic arithmetic uses the + and - kernel calls:

@example
(= gScore (+ gScore 10))                  @r{Addition}
(= gLivesLeft (- gLivesLeft 1))           @r{Subtraction}
(= gTotal (+ gWidth gHeight))             @r{Add two variables}
(= gFinalPrice (- gPrice gDiscount))      @r{Subtraction}
(= gDelta (- gNewValue gOldValue))        @r{Subtraction}
@end example

@node Arithmetic bit
@section Basic arithmetic: bit-shifting for mutiplication or division

Bit-shifting for multiplication/division by powers of two uses the << and >> operators (via * and / with powers of two):

@example
(= gResult (* gValue 4))                  @r{Left shift 2 bits (multiply by 4)}
(= gResult (/ gValue 2))                  @r{Right shift 1 bit (divide by 2)}
(= gResult (* gValue 8))                  @r{Multiply by 8}
(= gResult (/ gValue 16))                 @r{Divide by 16}
; Or using bitwise operators if available (not standard in SCI)
@end example

@node Complex arithmetic
@section Complex arithmetic: multiple expressions

Complex arithmetic expressions follow standard precedence rules:

@example
(= gTotal (+ (* gBaseSalary 12) gBonus (- gDeductions)))
(= gAverage (/ (+ gSum1 gSum2 gSum3) 3.0))
(= gDistance (sqrt (+ (sqr (- gX2 gX1)) (sqr (- gY2 gY1)))))
@end example

@node Boolean
@section Boolean expressions: equality and inequalities

Boolean expressions use logical operators and comparison operators:

@example
(if (> gScore 1000) ...)                 @r{Greater than}
(if (< gScore 50) ...)                   @r{Less than}
(if (= gScore 100) ...)                  @r{Equality}
(if (<> gScore 0) ...)                   @r{Inequality}
(if (and (>= gX 0) (< gX gMAX_X)) ...)   @r{Combined conditions}
(if (or (= gButton gBUTTON_A) (= gButton gBUTTON_B)) ...) @r{Alternative conditions}
@end example

@node If then else
@section Using if/then/else structures and examine/cond structures

Conditional execution uses if/then/else blocks:

@example
(if (> gTemperature gTHOT)
    (setq gStatus "HOT")
  (elseif (> gTemperature gTCOLD)
    (setq gStatus "WARM")
  (else
    (setq gStatus "COLD"))
  )

; SCI does not have a built-in case statement, but we can use if/elseif/else/endif
(if (or (= gAnswer "Y") (= gAnswer "y"))
    (print "YES")
  (elseif (or (= gAnswer "N") (= gAnswer "n"))
    (print "NO")
  (else
    (print "INVALID"))
  )
@end example

@node Performing
@section Performing loops

Loops are implemented using while, for, and foreach loops:

@example
; while loop equivalent (pre-test)
(= gI 1)
(while (<= gI 10)
    (Printf "Count: %d" gI)
    (= gI (+ gI 1))
)

; for loop equivalent (known iterations)
(for gI 1 10
    (Printf "Count: %d" gI)
)

; foreach loop equivalent (for lists)
(foreach gElem gList
    (Printf "%s" gElem)
)

; Loop with break and continue
(= gI 1)
(while (<= gI 100)
    (if (= (ListGet gArray gI) gTARGET_VALUE)
        (progn
          (Printf "Found at index: %d" gI)
          (break)))   @r{Exit loop immediately}
    (if (< (ListGet gArray gI) 0)
        (continue))   @r{Skip to next iteration}
    ; Process positive values
    (= gI (+ gI 1))
)
@end example

@node Breaking loops
@section Breaking out of loops safely

Early loop exit is achieved using break and continue statements:

@example
Exit while loop early
(= gI 1)
(while (<= gI 100)
    (if (= (ListGet gArray gI) gTARGET_VALUE)
        (progn
          (Printf "Found at index: %d" gI)
          (break)))   @r{Exit loop immediately}
    (= gI (+ gI 1))
)

; Skip current iteration
(= gI 1)
(while (<= gI 100)
    (if (< (ListGet gArray gI) 0)
        (continue))   @r{Skip to next iteration}
    ; Process non-negative values
    (= gI (+ gI 1))
)

; Nested loop control
(= gI 1)
(while (<= gI 10)
    (= gJ 1)
    (while (<= gJ 10)
        (if (= (ListGet (ListGet gMatrix gI) gJ) 0)
            (break))   @r{Exit inner loop only}
        ; Process non-zero elements
        (= gJ (+ gJ 1))
    )
    (= gI (+ gI 1))
)
@end example

@node Dialogue
@section Calling a dialogue script (Forth scripts)

SCI does not directly call Forth dialogue scripts. Instead, it uses
its own procedures. However, through the semantic-sugar
layer, SCI commands can be mapped to calls of the shared Fountain/Forth
runtime library:

@example
; Original SCI code
(if (and (> gX 100) (< gX 200))
    (ShowMessage gMSG_WELCOME)
  )

; Desugared via semantic-sugar layer
(if (and (> gX 100) (< gX 200))
    (:call-acc :target "ShowWelcomeMessage" :using nil)
  )
@end example

@node Tiles
@section Manipulating tiles

Tile manipulation is performed through direct video memory access
or through library routines for tiled backgrounds:

@example
; Direct memory access (mode-dependent)
; Assuming direct memory access via pointer
(= gVideoMemory #xb8000)
(= gOffset (* (+ (* gRow 80) gCol) 2))
(= (Peek gVideoMemory gOffset) gTileNum)
(= (Peek gVideoMemory (+ gOffset 1)) gAttribute)

; Library routine approach
(LoadTileSet gTILESET_ID)
(PlaceTile gLAYER gX gY gTILE_INDEX)
(ScrollBackground gDX gDY)
@end example

@node Decals
@section Manipulating decals

Decals (sprites or movable objects) are manipulated using:

@example
; Hardware sprite approach (if available)
(InitSprites)
(SetSpritePosition gSPRITE_ID gX gY)
(SetSpriteImage gSPRITE_ID gIMAGE_ID)
(ShowSprite gSPRITE_ID -1)   @r{-1 = visible}

; Software sprite approach
; Define a sprite structure
(struct sprite
  (x 0)
  (y 0)
  (frame 0)
)

; Create instance
(= gSprite (new sprite x 100 y 50 frame 0))

; Update
(= gSprite:x 100)
(= gSprite:y 50)
(= gSprite:frame (mod (+ gSprite:frame 1) 4))
@end example

@node Objects
@section Manipulating objects

Object manipulation depends on the implementation approach:

@example
; Define a game object class
(class CGameObject of BaseViewer
  (properties
    x 0
    y 0
    width 0
    height 0
    active #t)
  (method (init theX theY theWidth theHeight)
    (= x theX)
    (= y theY)
    (= width theWidth)
    (= height theHeight)
    (= active #t)
    (super init:))
  (method (move dx dy)
    (= x (+ x dx))
    (= y (+ y dy)))
)

; Create instances
(define gGameObjects (CreateArray 20))
(= (ArrayGet gGameObjects 0) (new CGameObject x 100 y 100 width 16 height 16))

; Update object position
(= gI 0)
(while (< gI 20)
    (Send (ArrayGet gGameObjects gI) 'move gDX gDY)
    (= gI (+ gI 1))
)

; Check active status and draw
(= gI 0)
(while (< gI 20)
    (if (Send (ArrayGet gGameObjects gI) 'isActive)
        (DrawObject (ArrayGet gGameObjects gI)))
    (= gI (+ gI 1))
)
@end example

@node Unsupported
@section Unsupported language features

The following SCI features are not supported in this EIGHTBOL release:

@table @asis
@item File I/O operations (Fopen, Fread, Fwrite, Fclose)
@item Graphics commands (DrawPic, Animate, ShowPic, etc.)
@item Hardware-specific features (Joystick, Mouse, Keyboard beyond basic)
@item Extended memory handling (unless emulated)
@item Multithreading or concurrent execution
@item Exception handling (unless via external interface)
@item Pointer arithmetic and manual memory management
@item Preprocessor directives (#define, #ifdef)
@item Object-oriented multiple inheritance (unless using external library)
@item Sound and music commands (unless using external library)
@end table

